------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TIER 1: STRONG DRIVERS (>5% variance explained)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DRIVER 1: Windspeed Shortfall (28.2% variance)
----------------------------------------------
Column needed: Mean_windspeed_self
Formula: ws_shortfall = max(WS_in_dataset) - Mean_windspeed_self

Description:
    Measures how much lower the sector's windspeed is compared to the
    highest windspeed in the dataset. Higher shortfall = lower wind sector.

Physical meaning:
    Low-wind sectors have higher relative errors due to three mechanisms:
    1. Mathematical amplification: Same absolute error → larger relative error
       when denominator (windspeed) is small
    2. Flow modeling limitations: Low-wind directions are often sheltered,
       with recirculation and complex flow that WAsP struggles to model
    3. Measurement uncertainty: Anemometer accuracy degrades below 3-4 m/s

Effect:
    γ = 0.349 ± 0.046
    σ multiplier: 1.42× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.30, 1.55]

Author's note:
    This emerged as the dominant driver, explaining more variance than
    traditional terrain metrics. Empirical analysis confirmed this captures
    real prediction difficulty, not just mathematical artifact:
    Q1 (low wind) sectors: 10.6% mean error
    Q4 (high wind) sectors: 5.9% mean error


DRIVER 2: Distance Score (22.9% variance)
-----------------------------------------
Column needed: distance_m, distance_A, distance_B
Formula: dist_score = max(distance_m - distance_A, 0) / (distance_B - distance_A)

Description:
    Measures how far apart the WTG and MM are, normalized by terrain-adjusted
    acceptable distance bounds. A score of 0 means the sites are very close
    (within the lower bound), while higher scores indicate greater separation.

Physical meaning:
    Wind conditions decorrelate over distance. The further apart two sites are,
    the less similar their wind climates will be, making cross-prediction
    less reliable at the sector level.

Effect:
    γ = 0.315 ± 0.064
    σ multiplier: 1.37× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.22, 1.54]

Author's note:
    Distance became much more important after removing problematic masts
    (jumped from 14% to 23% variance). Those masts were masking the true
    distance signal with data quality noise.


DRIVER 3: Turning Gradient (6.4% variance)
------------------------------------------
Column needed: d_turning_deg (for all 12 sectors)
Formula: For each sector, compute max(|current - left_neighbor|, |current - right_neighbor|)
         where neighbors wrap around (sector 12 neighbors sector 1)

Description:
    Measures how rapidly the wind deflection angle changes between adjacent
    sectors. High gradient = deflection changes sharply with wind direction.

Physical meaning:
    Sharp changes in deflection indicate complex terrain interactions that
    vary strongly with wind direction. The flow model must correctly capture
    these direction-dependent effects, which is challenging.

Effect:
    γ = 0.166 ± 0.064
    σ multiplier: 1.18× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.05, 1.33]


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TIER 2: MODERATE DRIVERS (0.5-5% variance explained)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DRIVER 4: Sector Sample Shortfall (4.8% variance)
-------------------------------------------------
Column needed: Sample_count_pred
Formula: sector_sample_shortfall = log(max_sector_samples + 1) - log(sector_samples + 1)

Description:
    Measures how few samples a sector has compared to the sector with most
    samples in the dataset. Higher shortfall = fewer measurements.

Physical meaning:
    Sectors with fewer samples (typically low-wind directions) have:
    - Less stable mean windspeed estimates
    - Higher actual prediction errors
    This is NOT purely epistemic - low-sample sectors genuinely have
    higher errors due to correlation with low windspeed and complex flow.

Effect:
    γ = 0.145 ± 0.070
    σ multiplier: 1.16× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.03, 1.33]

Deployment note:
    This feature uses absolute sample counts which scale differently in
    deployment (1 year) vs training (3-4 months). Consider using
    ws_shortfall as proxy, which captures similar signal without scaling issues.


DRIVER 5: |Δz| - Absolute Height Difference (1.7% variance)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: dz
Formula: |dz| = |WTG_elevation - MM_elevation|

Description:
    The magnitude of elevation difference between the WTG and MM locations,
    regardless of which is higher. Measured in meters.

Physical meaning:
    Large elevation differences mean wind conditions are affected differently
    by terrain at each location. The vertical structure of the wind profile
    differs, making horizontal extrapolation less reliable.

Effect:
    γ = 0.085 ± 0.055
    σ multiplier: 1.09× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.01, 1.23]

Author's note:
    |dz| is much more important for Energy Yield uncertainty (Rank #1 in EY model)
    than for Windspeed uncertainty. This is because EY errors compound through
    the power curve, while WS is a more direct measurement.


DRIVER 6: Log Energy Weight (0.9% variance)
-------------------------------------------
Column needed: weight_energy_predicted
Formula: log_energy_weight = log(weight_energy_predicted + ε)

Description:
    Log-transformed energy weight for the sector. Low values indicate
    low-energy sectors (infrequent wind directions).

Physical meaning:
    Low-energy sectors contribute less to overall production but often have
    higher relative errors. The log transformation captures the non-linear
    relationship between energy importance and prediction difficulty.

Effect:
    γ = 0.063 ± 0.052
    σ multiplier: 1.07× per +1 SD [95% CI: 1.00, 1.20]


